{"slug": "digital-logic-gates-explained", "targets": ["xand gate", "and using nor", "logic gates and or not"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">XAND Gate Meaning and How to Make AND Using NOR Gates</h2><p>A <strong>XAND gate</strong> is not one of the seven standard logic gates, and there is no standard IC with that name. When people search for XAND, they almost always mean the equivalence function, which is the XNOR gate: its output is 1 only when both inputs are equal (both 0 or both 1). Its expression is Y = AB + A'B'.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">A</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">B</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">XNOR (often called XAND)</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">AND</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1</td></tr></table></div><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">Steps to Build AND Using NOR</h3><ol><li>Feed input A to both inputs of NOR gate 1. Its output is A' (NOT A).</li><li>Feed input B to both inputs of NOR gate 2. Its output is B'.</li><li>Connect A' and B' to NOR gate 3. Its output is (A' + B')', which by De Morgan's theorem equals A.B.</li></ol><p>So an AND function needs three NOR gates. The same idea gives the basic <strong>logic gates AND, OR, NOT</strong> from NOR alone, which is why NOR is a universal gate.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Required gate</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">NOR gates needed</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">How</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">NOT</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Tie both inputs together</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">OR</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">2</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">NOR followed by a NOR inverter</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">AND</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">3</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Invert each input, then NOR them</td></tr></table></div>"}